From: Dave Wreski <dave@nic.com>
Subject: Re: Alpha vs. Dual Pentium II
In-Reply-To: <Pine.BSI.3.96.971217170141.11520Y-100000@get.wired.com>

>> Memory i/o and raw processing power may be bigger on the alpha side, but
>> as mail translates into many parallel low-bandwith processes that are
>> independent of each other, you should get substantial performance from a
>> dual-CPU system.

Doesn't that depend on the applications he will be using?  If I recall, mail
was his primary application, and sendmail, nor imap are most likely
configured to take advantage of dual-CPUs.

> Well, the machines tasks are not constrained just to handling mail, though
> most of what happens has _something_ to do with mail processing. 
> Potentially, we'll have over 300 users running IMAP on that machine within
> a few weeks.  Obviously, a sizable amount of RAM is required.  Either
> machine will be configured with hardware based RAID-0.  The machine that
> we're currently running on is a Pentium 180 running BSDI, which frequently
> becomes CPU bound.  Once the load on a machine that runs sendmail gets so
> high, it ceases mail processing until the load is back under control. 
> We're trying to avoid that with a hefty box that will scale easily. 

Why not consider:

1.  Using one box for outgoing, and one (or more) for incoming services
(mail and IMAP, specifically)

2.  Check out how the Sparc port is doing.  I believe among the supported
platformst, Sparc's will have the best I/O thruput..

Also, your machine stopping the delivery or processing of mail is due to a
configuration of sendmail, not an inability of BSD, in case that wasn't
clear.

Is the box currently I/O bound, memory bound, or CPU bound?  I realize you
said CPU bound, but how do you know?  Do you have 'iostat' to play with on
BSD?  How about vmstat?  I'm not sure that simply because 'top' reports a
high load average means its strictly CPU bound.

Dave

