On Sun, 21 Dec 1997, Andrew Smith wrote:

> Is any on using isdn with red hat 4.2?  I am thinking about making the jump
> and wanted to see if thier were any known issues with it and alpha linux. I
> have been using a 2.0.30 kernal and expect to continue to for a while.
> allso which isdn "modems. routers, termanal adapters" are prefered. it
> seems the terms are used interchangably.

Which is WRONG!

Modem:	Modulator/Demodulator

	Something that is NOT the case with ISDN. So we should abandon the
use of the term 'ISDN modem'. (Perhaps shoot on sight ;-)

Router:	A device routing on layer 3 (Check OSI reference model)

	Usually an IP router with an ISDN interface. The other side usualy
Ethernet if you are referring to the SOHO class routers. Physical
interfaces however have little to do with the fact wether or not something
is a router. A generic Linux system is also a router!

TA:	(Terminal Adapter)

	A device which adepts equipment to the ISDN network. These devices
usually act to the connected equipment (like your Linux system) as a modem
as they also accept AT style commands and so on. They should however NOT
be called ISDN modems! (I will shoot without futher warning on anyone on
this list abusing these terms again ;-)

Hope this message makes things more clear. If not make sure you visit the
ISDN pages of which I just don't have the URL at hand but .....

I found it with a simple search. Here it is:
http://www.alumni.caltech.edu/~dank/isdn/

Hugo.

